> For any topology $\T$ on $N$ define $p^*\T$ the topology of $M$ generated by $\T_M$ and the
> $\T_M$-connected components of $p^{-1}(\O)$ for $\O\in \T$. (l.1984)
>
> Let $\T$ be a (strong) thinner first countable and locally path connected topology on $N$ for
> which elements of $\Gamma(N/M)$ are continuous. […] Assume in addition there exists some
> $\R \subset \ord_p(N)$ strongly portly in $N$ on which strong and weak topologies agree. Then
> (a) $\widetilde M^S$ is strongly first countable connected locally path connected; (b) strong
> and weak topologies agree on $p^{-1}(\R)$; (c) $p^{-1}(\R)$ is strongly portly; (d) elements of
> $\Gamma(\widetilde M^S/M)$ are strongly continuous; (e) $\widetilde M^S\xrightarrow{p}N$ is a
> branched covering for the strong topologies. (l.1990–2004)
>
> Intuitively, if $(M,\T_M)\xrightarrow{p}(N,\T_N)$ is a spread and $\T$ is thinner than $\T_N$,
> then the strong topology $p^*\T$ is the coarsest topology on $M$ such that
> $(M,p^*\T)\xrightarrow{p}(N,\T)$ is a spread. However, the definition does not garantee that
> $\T_M$-connected components are $p^*\T$-connected components. Point $(e)$ above is thus
> non-trivial. (l.2006)
>
> Hypothesis (C) for the model spaces is necessary for the proof. Take
> $\theta \in \RR_+\setminus \{0,2\pi\}$ and consider $\mass{\theta}$ as a singular model. We may
> choose as tamifying topology either the natural one or the tamifying topology $\T$ we defined on
> $\mass{0}$. […] We may thus construct a messed up tamifying topology on
> $\widetilde \E_\theta^{1,2}$ by taking the topology $\T'$ generated by the natural one and the
> restriction of $\T$ to $\{t>0\}$. Since the Galois group acts by 'rotation' around the 'axis'
> $\{r=0\}$ it preserves $\T'$. However, the automorphism group also contains translations along
> the $t$-axis which send points from $t<0$ to $t>0$. (l.2137–2142)
>
> Let $M$ be a virtually tamely singular $(G,X)$-manifold. If $M$ admits an injective developing
> map $N\xrightarrow{\D}X$ for some branched covering $N\rightarrow M$ possibly branched above
> $\sing(M)$ then $M$ is isomorphic to $\Gamma(N/M)\backslash\D(N)$ where $\Gamma(N/M)$ acts on $X$
> via the holonomy of $\D$; $N$ is endowed with the quotient of a tamifying topology $\T$; $\D(N)$
> is endowed with the topology generated by that of $X$ and the images by $\D$ of $\T$. (l.2154)

